{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Detection worked, end to end — recording it with real latencies, because this outage is a natural\ntest of the controls epic 6202 and `bff5-9163` put in place, and it passed.\n\n    01:33 UTC   Reconcile Bridge run #1 fails (ACLI 1200-issue ceiling)\n                -> publishes rebar/host:bridge_run_failures = 1\n    02:09 UTC   Reconciler Heartbeat Canary goes RED   (+36 min)\n                \"Reconciler is stale — Reconciler unhealthy — canonical status is FAILED\"\n    02:34 UTC   Reconcile Bridge run #2 fails\n                -> publishes bridge_run_failures = 1 (second consecutive)\n    ~02:50 UTC  rebar-bridge-run-failures alarm  OK -> ALARM   (+77 min)\n\nBoth independent controls fired, neither needed a human, and the 36-minute canary latency is its\nscheduled cadence (it runs roughly 2-hourly; last green 00:19, first red 02:09), not sluggishness.\n\n## Why this is worth writing down\n\nWhen I first saw `rebar-bridge-run-failures` sitting at OK during a known outage, I nearly filed it\nas a live dead-man inversion. It was not. The alarm requires TWO consecutive failures by design,\nand a healthy run publishes an explicit `0` rather than silence — which is exactly what makes\n`notBreaching` safe on this one alarm. That reasoning was recorded on `bff5-9163` before this\noutage progressed, and the outage then confirmed it independently within the hour.\n\nThe residual case that alarm deliberately does NOT cover — \"the workflow stopped running at all,\nso nothing is published\" — is owned by the Reconciler Heartbeat Canary, per\n`monitoring_bridge.tf:63-64`. That is the control that fired first here, 36 minutes in. So the\ndivision of responsibility documented in the terraform is not aspirational; it executed.\n\n## The one thing detection did NOT do\n\nIt did not tell anyone WHY. Both signals say \"the reconciler is unhealthy\"; neither names the\n1200-issue ceiling. That came from opening the workflow log. That is a reasonable division — an\nalarm should page, not diagnose — but it is worth noting that the remediation text in\n`monitoring_bridge.tf:58-61` points at `JIRA_API_TOKEN` expiry as \"the most common cause\", which\nis NOT the cause here. An operator following the runbook would have checked a valid token first.\nWorth a small runbook amendment alongside the fix in change 2657."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788581976528785001,"uuid":"8b73194b-72f5-4b0b-ba3c-aea92ba837e8"}